{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>A soul, when it sins and commits a trespass, etc.</b> One needs to know why it says כִּי תֶחֱטָא, for we cannot explain here that the sin is the denial, since it goes on to say ״וְכִחֵשׁ בַּעֲמִיתוֹ״. It further says ״מַעַל בַ־ה׳״ — something not said regarding any other sins. It further says ״וְכִחֵשׁ בַּעֲמִיתוֹ״, which should not have needed to say ״בַּעֲמִיתוֹ״. Also, the precise phrasing of the formulation is not exact." "<b>Or perhaps</b> the verse informs us of three branches of wickedness that the perpetrator of this transgression commits. The first is the taking of something that is not his — the branch of robbery. Second — ״וּמָעֲלָה מַעַל בַ־ה׳״, meaning that in HaShem, as it were, he committed the trespass — meaning the language of alteration (שִׁנּוּי), for He judged according to the deeds and vindicated [the victim] that this money should be his, and when this one comes and robs him, it turns out that he causes a trespass (מְעִילָה) in HaShem, for He altered the judgment upon him — that what had reached him according to the judgment of HaShem did not remain in his hand, and HaShem is, as it were, suspected of not having done just judgment. Or perhaps the reason He doubled the expression, saying ״וּמָעֲלָה מַעַל״ and did not suffice with ״וּמָעֲלָה בַ־ה׳״, is that He intended to include also the aspect of the robber, for even from his side he has trespassed against HaShem, as it were, for He [HaShem] judged regarding the loss of the acquired thing, and he [the robber] takes what was not decreed upon him by the judgments of HaShem. Third — ״וְכִחֵשׁ בַּעֲמִיתוֹ״, meaning that he makes the righteous man appear wicked: when he [the victim] demands his deposit back, etc., he [the robber] says to him, ״Such things never happened,״ and makes him out to be a liar — and this is what the verse says: ״וְכִחֵשׁ בַּעֲמִיתוֹ״, meaning he places the lie (כִּחוּשׁ) upon his fellow, who is the one robbed from — he makes him out to be wicked, demanding something that is not his and saying to him, ״Why do you lie about me?״ and contending with him. Now, these two things [the trespass against HaShem and the lie against one's fellow] apply only to a deposit, or to an item left in one's trust, or to robbery when one robs him of what is his, or to withholding a hired worker's wages — but in the case of finding a lost object there is no element of ״כִּחֵשׁ בַּעֲמִיתוֹ״. Therefore the verse states separately, ״אוֹ מָצָא אֲבֵדָה וְכִחֶשׁ בָהּ״, etc." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes to three evil things caused by the guilty soul. The first: it is diminished of its light and its excellence — and this is what the verse says, ״נֶפֶשׁ כִּי תֶחֱטָא״ — the language of deficiency (חִסָּרוֹן) in the soul itself. Second — ״וּמָעֲלָה מַעַל בַ־ה׳״, in the manner stated by the men of truth: there is no soul in Israel whose spiritual influx (שֶׁפַע) of the soul does not descend to it to sustain it, for without this it has no vitality. [Without the influx] of the soul that ascended — whose whiteness (צַחֲנָתָהּ) and goodness (בָאְשָׁהּ) it corrupted in grievous sin — therefore the divine influx is cut off from it, as it says (Genesis 17:14): ״וְנִכְרְתָה הַנֶּפֶשׁ הַהִיא.״ And the path of the influx comes through two fine threads, through the two nostrils — and this is the path of the place of the soul's cleaving to its Maker, as it says (Deuteronomy 4:4): ״וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים בַ־ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם.״" "<b>Now behold,</b> when a person sins with sins such as these, the [evil] forces rule over that soul and its external influxes, as is known, and those wicked ones derive benefit from the divine influx. And this is what the verse says: ״וּמָעֲלָה מַעַל בַ־ה׳״ — as it were, in the light drawn from Him [HaShem]. And this is the sod alluded to in the verse (Proverbs 28:24): ״גּוֹזֵל אָבִיו וְאִמּוֹ, etc. — חָבֵר הוּא לְאִישׁ מַשְׁחִית״, meaning that to the extent he associates with the aspect of evil called אִישׁ מַשְׁחִית, understand this." "<b>The third</b> is that he also causes a denial (הַכְחָשָׁה) to the collective of the community — meaning, because all Israel are as one, the evil of one individual brings evil upon the entire offshoot. And this is what the verse says: ״וְכִחֵשׁ בַּעֲמִיתוֹ״ — for his fellow is made a denier (כְּחוּשָׁה) from his side, so to speak, from on high. And this is what is alluded to in the verse (Proverbs 28:24): ״גּוֹזֵל אָבִיו וְאִמּוֹ,״ and they of blessed memory said (Berakhot 35a): אִמּוֹ — this is Knesset Israel, for through the merit of men of valor the peace, the good, and the life increase in the collective of the people of HaShem." "<b>Thus concludes the parashah of Vayikra.</b>"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {2 "I read בָאְשָׁהּ (her goodness) literally per the Hebrew text, though some editions have variant readings for this word. The root ב־א־שׁ meaning 'goodness/excellence' is unusual but is retained faithfully."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:53:53.850982884Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}